Package: lindos-tune
Version: 1.0.0
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Architecture: all
Maintainer: Lindos Team <team@lindos.dev>
Depends: python3 (>= 3.10), lindos-core (>= 1.0.0), systemd, procps, util-linux, zram-tools | systemd-zram-generator, earlyoom, lm-sensors
Recommends: power-profiles-daemon, ananicy-cpp, fancontrol, hdparm, pciutils, kmod
Suggests: tlp, thermald, nbfc-linux, gamemode
Homepage: https://lindos.dev
Description: Lindos RAM / performance / hardware tuning (lindos-tune)
 The tuning layer of Lindos (SPEC §11): keeps the desktop idling at the
 350–500 MB RAM target (Lite: 300–380 MB) and gives one CLI for the knobs
 that matter on a Windows-style Linux desktop:
  * lindos-tune status   — RAM used/available, zram, top-10 RSS, enabled
                           units, governor, compositor and a verdict line
                           against the idle target (--json for tools).
  * lindos-tune apply    — base tune + per-mode overrides (Everyday / Gaming /
                           Work / Creator / Lite): sysctl (swappiness for zram,
                           BBR, cache pressure), zram (systemd-zram-generator or
                           zram-tools, auto-detected), earlyoom, journald cap,
                           /tmp on tmpfs, systemd presets that disable
                           bluetooth/ModemManager/cups-browsed/apport/whoopsie/
                           kerneloops/brltty/speech-dispatcher/NM-wait-online
                           (never purged; cups stays socket-activated, avahi
                           stays on), autostart hiding, CPU governor + EPP,
                           ananicy-cpp in Gaming, fstrim.timer.  Idempotent,
                           chroot/offline safe (used by the ISO build).
  * lindos-tune services — list / enable / disable a whitelisted set of units.
  * lindos-tune zram, governor, power, fan — zram size, cpufreq governor
                           (power-profiles-daemon when present), power profile
                           (powerprofilesctl → tlp → governor), fan profiles
                           (nbfc-linux / thinkpad_acpi; honest "no controllable
                           fans" otherwise) and lm-sensors readouts.
  * lindos-tune report   — Markdown bug report (RAM, zram, top RSS, units,
                           kernel, mode, GPU, managed config files).
 .
 Privileged operations run through the lindos-core helper (pkexec
 /usr/libexec/lindos/lindos-helper); lindos-tune never calls sudo itself.
 RAM savings listed in /usr/share/lindos/tune/ram-budget.json are estimates
 until measured; the stock Mint 22 XFCE baseline is roughly 600–750 MB.
